This paper investigates whether a passively oriented, spin-stabilised solar sail in near-polar low Earth orbit can exhibit a bounded interval of net orbital-energy gain through eclipse-modulated solar-radiation-pressure (SRP) forcing. Using a reduced analytical model and three-dimensional numerical propagations, we show that such a regime does exist, but only within a narrow region of LTDN--pitch space. At \(700\,\mathrm{km}\), only \(89\) of \(900\) sampled cases (\(\sim 10\%\)) exhibit positive semi-major-axis excursion within the \(14\)-day analysis window, with the strongest case reaching \(\Delta a \approx +20.07\,\mathrm{km}\) at LTDN \(12.266\,\mathrm{h}\) and pitch \(24.8^\circ\). Eclipse is shown to be causally decisive: for the representative gain case, the eclipse-on trajectory reaches \(\Delta a \approx +10.31\,\mathrm{km}\) by day \(14\), whereas the eclipse-off counterpart falls to \(\Delta a \approx -47.75\,\mathrm{km}\). Orbit-resolved SRP work diagnostics show that gain occurs only when eclipse suppresses predominantly negative-work portions of the SRP cycle. Longer-horizon propagation shows that this effect is transient and self-limiting rather than sustained. The representative gain case reaches a peak semi-major axis of \(7098.211\,\mathrm{km}\) at day \(26.50\), but declines to \(7064.717\,\mathrm{km}\) by day \(75\), below its initial value of \(7078.137\,\mathrm{km}\). The early increase in semi-major axis is driven mainly by apogee growth rather than uniform orbit raising, together with perigee erosion and eccentricity growth, which increase drag exposure and weaken the favourable SRP projection. The paper therefore establishes a narrower result: eclipse timing can enable a real but bounded transient energy-pumping regime in near-polar LEO, but the same coupled dynamics that permit the early gain also drive its later breakdown.
